%description:

RFC 768 — the length field counts the header and the data, so its minimum is eight, and a
receiver accepts a datagram of that size.

Checks RFC768-HDR-3 (description), the minimum, from
doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc768/catalog.md. The English procedure is in
doc/project/evidence/protocol/udp/checks/delivery.md#empty-datagram; the program steps below
map onto its expected observations. Mapping of the mockup with a relay: host A = hostA,
gateway R = router, relay T = tap, host B = hostB.

The level 2 pass could not reach this observation. No sending program in the model can build
a datagram with no data, because a chunk of length zero is refused
(Packet::insertAt, src/inet/common/packet/Packet.cc), and the level 2 results recorded the
gap and left it for the level 3 toolset. This test uses that toolset: the relay takes a
normal datagram and removes its data, so that an 8-octet datagram reaches host B without a
sender that could build one.

The relay sets the UDP checksum to zero at the same time, which RFC 768 reads as "the
transmitter generated no checksum". A value that covered the old data would be wrong for the
new datagram, and the receiver would then discard it for the checksum and not answer the
question this check asks. See UdpMutations.h, emptyUdpDatagram.

Expected result: FAIL, and the reason is not the UDP module. The run shows that UDP accepted
the 8-octet datagram and handed zero octets to the program: `Sending payload up to socket
sockId=1`, and `UdpSink: Received packet: ... (0 B) ... 10.0.0.1:4000 --> 10.0.0.6:5000`.
The run then stopped with a runtime error, before the tester could record the verdict:

    Error: Returning an empty chunk is not allowed according to the flags: 0 while
    processing statistic signal 'packetReceived' ... emitted from (inet::UdpSink)

The receiving program declares `@statistic[endToEndDelay](source="dataAge(packetReceived)")`
(src/inet/applications/udpapp/UdpSink.ned:36). That filter is
DataAgeFilter::receiveSignal (src/inet/common/ResultFilters.cc), which calls
`packet->peekData()` with no flags; for a packet of zero length the call returns an empty
chunk, which is refused (EmptyChunk.h:51). A datagram of the smallest size RFC 768 allows
therefore stops a simulation whose receiver is a standard application.

The statistic stays on, and the scenario does not turn it off. Turning it off would make the
test pass and would hide the finding: what a user of the model meets is exactly this error.
A runtime error is a FAIL for the test runner. See doc/project/evidence/model/udp/results.md.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "UdpMutations.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    // The relay removes the data and leaves the 8-octet header.
    return ProtocolTest("rfc768_empty_datagram")
        .intercept(intercept("tap").match("udp.destPort == 5000").nth(1)
                     .mutate([](Packet *frame) { emptyUdpDatagram(frame); })
                     .describe("the datagram (remove its data, leaving the 8-octet header)"))
        // Expected observation 1: the datagram on link 2 before the relay carries 100 octets of
        // data, so the change below is visible against it.
        .once(on("router.eth[1].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .packet("udp.destPort == 5000 && udp.totalLengthField == 108B")
                  .describe("the datagram on link 2 before the relay, 108 octets long")
                  .within(0.5))
        // Expected observation 2: at host B's interface the length field is eight, the minimum
        // (RFC768-HDR-3).
        .once(on("hostB.eth[0].mac").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .packet("udp.destPort == 5000 && udp.totalLengthField == 8B")
                  .describe("the datagram at host B's interface with a length field of eight")
                  .within(0.1))
        // Expected observation 3: host B's UDP hands zero octets upward -- it accepted the
        // smallest datagram the format allows.
        .once(on("hostB.udp").signal("packetSentToUpper")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) { return c.event.packet->getDataLength() == B(0); })
                  .describe("zero octets handed up from host B's UDP toward the program")
                  .within(0.1));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc768_empty_datagram compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.node.inet.Router;
import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;
import inet.protocoltest.PacketTap;

// The mockup with a relay: hostA -- router -- tap -- hostB.
network Rfc768TapNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        hostA: StandardHost { @display("p=200,100"); }
        router: Router { @display("p=350,100"); }
        tap: PacketTap { @display("p=500,100"); }
        hostB: StandardHost { @display("p=650,100"); }
    connections:
        hostA.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> router.ethg++; // link 1 = router.eth[0]
        router.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> tap.a;        // link 2 = router.eth[1], before the relay
        tap.b <--> Eth100M <--> hostB.ethg++;         // link 2, after the relay
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = Rfc768TapNet
sim-time-limit = 1s
seed-set = 0

# Static ARP keeps the wire free of ARP request/reply noise.
**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

# Every node computes the IPv4 header checksum, so the relay's rewrite leaves a valid one.
**.ipv4.ip.checksumMode = "computed"

# One UDP datagram, 100 octets, to port 5000 on host B.
*.hostA.numApps = 1
*.hostA.app[0].typename = "UdpBasicApp"
*.hostA.app[0].destAddresses = "hostB"
*.hostA.app[0].destPort = 5000
*.hostA.app[0].localPort = 4000
*.hostA.app[0].messageLength = 100B
*.hostA.app[0].startTime = 0.1s
*.hostA.app[0].sendInterval = 1s
*.hostB.numApps = 1
*.hostB.app[0].typename = "UdpSink"
*.hostB.app[0].localPort = 5000

%# expected-result: FAIL
%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc768_empty_datagram: PASS
